{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>This day HaShem your Elohim commands you, etc.</b> After your own mouth has testified concerning you that to HaShem belongs the world and its fullness, and from His hand in blessing He has given you everything — from now on you must accept upon yourself to keep the commandments of HaShem. ״You have declared HaShem this day, etc., and HaShem has declared you this day, etc.״ — for you declared Him in these declarations of first fruits and tithes, and you said, ״I have hearkened to the voice of HaShem,״ and corresponding to this, HaShem has also declared you, to look down from His holy habitation from the heavens and to bless you with surpassing exaltation above all the peoples. Even though all looking-down is for evil, nevertheless with you the matter changes like a Segullah-potion that has no rational explanation. This is what it says: ״And HaShem has declared you this day to be to Him for a treasured people (לְעַם סְגֻלָּה).״ And some say that even this looking-down is for evil, and its meaning is: look down upon the nations that provoke you, and make them a ransom for your people Israel. Therefore it does not say ״look down to bless,״ but rather ״and bless (וּבָרֵךְ)״ is a word standing on its own. And according to this approach it is well said: ״And HaShem has declared you this day to be to Him for a treasured people, etc., and to make you highest above all the nations״ — for this matter is explained through the ״looking-down״ by which HaShem has declared us. And even if ״you declared (הֶאֱמַרְתָּ)״ and ״He declared you (הֶאֱמִירְךָ)״ are an expression of adorning (ברכות ו.), nevertheless it is explained according to this approach. And from the fact that it says in both of them the expression ״this day,״ infer from it that it refers to the parashah above, of first fruits and tithes, for in them these two declarations are explained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The reference (ברכות ו.) is to Bava Batra 6a, where האמרה/האמיר is glossed as לשון חטיבה (adornment); I have preserved the citation as the author wrote it. The word סגולה carries a double sense in this comment — 'treasured people' in the verse and 'potion/charm' in the parable — which I have tried to keep visible by rendering it 'Segullah-potion' in the derash context while leaving the verse translation as 'treasured people.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:10.217119587Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}